{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "085673d9-6167-5c0c-b43c-bc1e085569fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311560 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\, \\\\ \\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$  Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45874040-9e7b-5fb5-9a00-ef344d9b3cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311794 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Starting from $\\text{L3},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwe evaluate both sides at $t={t}_{0}$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ $\\text{by substituting }t={t}_{0}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can use the property \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7},$ we get line $\\text{L8}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186391587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391586 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-1) \\\\ y(-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6580aa1b-3c6f-5533-9e32-e7afb350be95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391586 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-(-1))}\\,x(-1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1)) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186391592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186391592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391587 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "36126579-e63c-590b-a6f7-f58bde2b64a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391588 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t+1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t+1))\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186391588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863